proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "When several highlighted cubes shared a color, the recorded pickups progressed from robot-right to robot-left, whereas the stipulated increasing-world-x order assigned the count to different cubes. After a lifted cube was returned, the same count consequently selected an already-processed cube or the wrong remaining cube. This breaks SUFFICIENCY and makes the stipulated identity-based count update inconsistent with the observed pickups, breaking PREDICTABILITY. Replace the target ordering with red, green, blue, omitting unhighlighted colors, and within each color increasing world y, then increasing world x for ties. Determine this fixed order from highlight_frame, not from positions changed by handling. Keep the field as the number of distinct targets in this ordered prefix whose completed pickup lift has been observed. Initialize to 0; increment when the next target is visibly held off the table with TCP approximately z=0.15, not when grasping or commanding a lift. Preserve it through return, release, and retreat, and never count the same target again. For example, 2 means the first two targets in this corrected order have completed their lifts.",
      "value_type": "count"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "With multiple highlighted cubes of one color, the old increasing-world-x order disagreed with the observed robot-right-to-left pickup order. Consequently a carried count could exclude the wrong identities after release and retreat, breaking SUFFICIENCY and PREDICTABILITY. Recover the fixed target order from highlight_frame: red, green, blue, omitting unhighlighted colors, then increasing world y within each color, with increasing world x only as a tie-breaker. Do not reorder using positions changed by handling. Initialize lifted_count to 0. Increment the carried value once only when this cycle visibly shows the next target in that fixed order held off the table after a lift with TCP approximately z=0.15. Use the current motion frames and held-cube identity to distinguish that new completion from a previously counted cube still being held. Do not increment for a low grasp, a commanded lift, an empty grasp, or an action executing no steps. Preserve the count through lowering, release, and retreat. For example, 2 excludes the first two targets in the corrected order from further pickup."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Two systematic failures need correction. First, while approaching the button with button_pressed=false, the achieved hover TCP was displaced from the button center by roughly 1–1.5 cm. The policy closed at that displaced TCP with an upward nudge, then pressed using the same displaced x,y. The visible highlights had correctly been saved, but they did not establish a button press. This is a SUFFICIENCY failure in the geometric action rules, not missing historical information. Explicitly distinguish the achieved TCP from the object-centered waypoint. Before closing at hover and again before pressing, estimate the button center from the current calibrated views, using the wrist image to refine it. With open fingers at approximate button hover, command that button-centered x,y at z=0.15 and close on arrival. With closed fingers at approximate hover and no observed contact, descend directly to the newly estimated button-centered x,y at z=0.0075, keeping closed. Being approximately above the button permits the next operation; it does not imply that the achieved TCP x,y equals the button center. Retain contact recognition near z=0.024 and the closed retraction followed by opening. Apply the minimum-translation safeguard only after constructing the object-centered waypoint: if it already provides at least 2 mm translation, do not add an upward nudge; otherwise add a small upward adjustment within 5 mm. Remove any interpretation of 'same nominal approach waypoint' as copying the achieved TCP.\n\nSecond, after the highlights disappeared, multiple same-color targets were selected in an order inconsistent with the recorded pickups. After counted cubes were returned, this also led to regrasping them or redirecting away from the correctly approached next cube. This breaks SUFFICIENCY because lifted_count was interpreted against the wrong target order. Replace increasing-world-x same-color ordering everywhere with increasing world y, then increasing world x for ties, while retaining red-green-blue color priority. Establish this fixed order from highlight_frame and match individual cubes by position as well as color; robot-right is negative y and appears on the left of the front image. Interpret lifted_count as excluding the completed prefix of this order, including a newly completed lift recognized from this cycle. Select the next uncounted identity before testing whether the hand is aligned for descent. An open hover above a returned, counted cube must lead to an open approach to the next target at z=0.15, not a new grasp. Permit descent to z=0.02 and closure only when the open hand is approximately aligned over that selected uncounted target, allowing the existing approach-error tolerance. If already aligned over that target, descend rather than issuing another lateral hover. Keep the existing low-grasp lift, counted-cube return, open vertical retreat, and final-target holding rules. No additional memory field or generic preference for whichever highlighted cube happens to be beneath the hand is needed."
}

